{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And they said, 'The land is good which YHWH our Elohim gives to us.'</b> Who are they that spoke well of it? Joshua and Caleb — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 1:25) from Sifrei (Deuteronomy 25). And I am astonished: if so, what is this accusation against them, for it would be fitting to believe the ten more than the two. Perhaps he argued against them: since HaKadosh Barukh Hu made it known to you that it is a good land, and the great ones among the emissaries and the righteous of them also testify accordingly, you should have believed that the others spoke as they did out of faintness of heart. But the correct interpretation is what I explained there (Numbers 13:32), that all of them acknowledged before Moses and Aaron and all the congregation and said (Numbers 13:27), 'We came to the land to which you sent us, and it also flows with milk and honey, and this is its fruit' — and so they were all of one accord regarding the goodness of the land, but the strength of those peoples melted their hearts. And afterwards, when they saw that Joshua and Caleb strengthened their hearts for battle, the rest of the spies came to the children of Israel not in the presence of Moses and brought out an evil report about the land, that it is a land that devours its inhabitants, and this is 'and you murmured in your tents' (Deuteronomy 1:27). And moreover, the people did not mention this report before Moses, for he would have refuted them — that they had not said so at first, and had acknowledged that it was good and also flows with milk and honey — but they said to Moses (Deuteronomy 1:28), 'Where are we going up? Our brothers melted our hearts, saying: a people greater and taller than we.' And therefore Moses says to them, 'It is true according to your words' — that that people is great and tall — 'but YHWH will fight for you, according to all that He did for you from Egypt until now.' And this is the meaning of 'and in this matter you do not believe in YHWH your Elohim' — that the only hindrance is from a lack of faith. Behold, the entire passage according to its plain sense and meaning:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:13:55.992890940Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}